import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "2 Peter",
  display: "Second Peter",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 01"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"In the last book of Peter, we saw Peter give a very candid look at how to find joy during a very difficult season. We must remember that Peter is penning this just before his own death by crucifixion. It is likely that he was experiencing harsh trials himself, which is why he knew exactly how to act, or react, during difficulty, and which is more than likely why he found it important to reach out to his fellow churches to lift them up for the same issues that he was facing."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems (while going through the letters of 1, & 2 Peter, as well as 1, 2, 3 John, and 1, 2 Timothy, they all seem to address the same issues. Being fervent in the faith amongst a people who are acting like allies. 1, 2 Timothy gives ample description of what to do as a leader in the church so that people are not led astray. 1, 2, 3 John gives us encouragement to look at the people around us and be cautious about false preaching. 1, & 2 Peter give us the evidence of false teaching among the leadership."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter, in this book particularly, helps give the reader food for thought when continuing through trials, and how to navigate ministry to a people who will ultimately try and hurt you. We must be constantly prepared for backstabbing and backlash if we are going to serve the Lord in any fashion and in any church. Still love the people around you, but still be watching who you pour into, and who pours into you."}],"previously":["In the last letter of Peter, we saw Peter give a very candid look at how to find joy during a very difficult season. We must remember that Peter is penning this just before his own death by crucifixion. It is likely that he was experiencing harsh trials himself, which is why he knew exactly how to act or react during difficulty, and which is more than likely why he found it important to reach out to his fellow churches to lift them up for the same issues that he was facing. It seems (while going through the letters of 1 & 2 Peter, as well as 1, 2, & 3 John, and 1 & 2 Timothy) they all address the same issues. Being fervent in faith amongst a people who are acting like allies. 1 & 2 Timothy gives ample description of what to do as a leader in the church so that people are not led astray. 1, 2, & 3 John gives us encouragement to look at the people around us and be cautious about false preaching. 1 & 2 Peter gives us the evidence of false teaching among the leadership. Peter, in this book particularly, helps give the reader food for thought when continuing through trials, and how to navigate ministry to a people who will ultimately try and hurt you. We must be constantly prepared for backstabbing and backlash if we are going to serve the Lord in any fashion and in any church. Still love the people around you, but still be watching who you pour into, and who pours into you."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Greeting","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter uses that strange word again, “precious.” This time he is calling our faith of righteousness “precious.” This is how we can genuinely know it is the apostle Peter writing this letter, in case you don’t believe his opening acclimation calling himself “Simon Peter, a servant and an apostle of Jesus Christ…”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Notice, though, that Peter (being an Apostle of Christ, and quite frankly more than appropriately can call himself a leader of the apostles, or even of the church) doesn’t call himself a leader, but rather a “servant.” He’s letting it be known that he is on an equal playing field with the rest of the church and should not be looked at as anything higher than a minister. 2 Grace and peace be multiplied unto you through the knowledge of God, and of Jesus our Lord,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Grace and peace are always prayed for in the saints and in the congregation. We should all desire that the world finds and is multiplied in “grace and peace,” and in that specific order. We cannot have the peace of God until we have the grace of God."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":15,"title":"Confirming Your Calling","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"May we be reminded that it is only by God’s power that we have everything we need to live this Christian life. We would be completely useless and lost without it. God has given us power above anything the world can have."},{"kind":"para","text":"Take something as significant as sin, for instance. We have the power that no one else in the world has. We have the power to reject and overcome sin. The people in this world do not have that power because they do not know the one who has given the power to overcome sin. 4 Whereby are given unto us exceeding great and precious promises: that by these ye might be partakers of the divine nature, having escaped the corruption that is in the world through lust."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s the “promises” of God that are counted as precious and exceeding great. What exceeds great? There have been many in history who were labeled as “great” by worldly definition. They even have “great” in their title. We’ve all heard of “Alexander the Great,” “Henry the Great,” “Constantine the Great,” or even someone like “The Great Houdini.” They were great by a certain standard for their valiant efforts and leadership in some areas."},{"kind":"para","text":"God promises, which have been found to be in the divine nature of God, allowing us power to escape corruption as Peter labels here, promise to exceed great. We don’t even have a word for it! It’s above any worldly measurement of what we can even imagine as “great.” The world’s most costly treasures can’t even compare to the promises of God. 5 And beside this, giving all diligence, add to your faith virtue; and to virtue knowledge; 6 And to knowledge temperance; and to temperance patience; and to patience godliness; 7 And to godliness brotherly kindness; and to brotherly kindness charity."},{"kind":"para","text":"I can go down a whole list of use cases for these verses (in fact, I have in past lessons). Peter demonstrates here something we can “add” to our faith! There is a lot of value in these labeled additions to faith. Faith is the stepping stone to growing closer to God, and then we must add these things in order to stay within the faith parameters of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"He says to add “virtue” to your faith. “Virtue” means “moral goodness, modesty, and purity.” If we are going to have valuable faith, we must add modesty to it. We cannot be seen in our faith as prideful."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then to virtue, we are told to add knowledge. Not specifically wisdom, but knowledge. People tend to get these two words mixed up. Solomon speaks very strongly about the differences between knowledge and wisdom."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A wise man is strong; yea, a man of knowledge increaseth strength.","ref":"Proverbs 24:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you are wise, you will be strong, but if you are knowledgeable, you will know how to get stronger."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The heart of the prudent getteth knowledge; and the ear of the wise seeketh knowledge.","ref":"Proverbs 18:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge: but fools despise wisdom and instruction.","ref":"Proverbs 1:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"But then Solomon looks at wisdom as a connected source to knowledge, but not knowledge itself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Happy is the man that findeth wisdom, and the man that getteth understanding.","ref":"Proverbs 3:13"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wisdom is the principal thing; therefore get wisdom: and with all thy getting get understanding.","ref":"Proverbs 4:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"Wisdom is a person; it is the embodiment of Jesus Christ. This is a highlight throughout all of Proverbs. If you know Christ, then you know wisdom. From there, you must acquire knowledge and understanding."},{"kind":"para","text":"I would say it like this: knowledge is learning; wisdom is proven. A friend once said, “Knowledge is knowing that a tomato is a fruit. Wisdom is knowing not to put it in a fruit salad.” If we are going to add humility and moral goodness to our faith, we must have knowledge and continual growth in our virtue."},{"kind":"para","text":"He doesn’t stop there, though. He advises adding temperance to our knowledge before adding it to our virtue to increase our strength. “Temperance” means to have “self-control.” I don’t like to use the term “self” in any matter of faith. That might be too Calvinistic of a thought, but the truth of the matter is, faith is by God, and it’s for God. We cannot have faith without God, and so it’s not of our own doing that we have faith, but by the fact that God has revealed the truth to us. Therefore, I would rather put “temperance” as “obeying your master’s control.” We should not be in the position of having control of anything because we are sinful and arrogant people (no offense). So we must give control to the Lord. Temperance is rejecting our control and accepting God’s control. That’s honestly the only way to obtain knowledge, which is what we are supposed to be adding our temperance to."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle, and easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy.","ref":"James 3:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"Wisdom and knowledge must come from understanding God first. Otherwise, we are building our hearts and rewiring our brains based on something unstable. After we have added temperance to knowledge, we are told to add patience to temperance. Honestly, I don’t need to say much about this because we all know what patience means. However, I think I can accurately assess and say that we all struggle the most in this area. We live in an age of impatience. We can’t wait for anything."},{"kind":"para","text":"We literally have drive-thrus for speedy service, internet with quick and simple answers to literally any question, groceries delivered to our house on the same day we order them, and we complain if our packages take an extra day to get here than they said it would. We are a spoiled people, which has caused us to become an impatient people. Patience is a very important mark to make in your Bibles because it’s the area that we need to grow in the most. Peter is even arguing to say that you cannot have temperance without patience. This means you can’t have knowledge, or virtue, or even faith unless we are first attuned to our need and honed in on our necessity of patience."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience. But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing.","ref":"James 1:3-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many verses that highlight the importance of patience, such as Romans 15:4, 2 Corinthians 6:4, Colossians 1:11, 1 Thessalonians 1:3, Hebrews 6:12, and Romans 5:3-5. Peter advises us to add godliness to patience, emphasizing the need to be controlled by God’s viewpoint in all matters, including our patience. To godliness, we must also add brotherly love, which is often lacking in many churches today. However, this can be addressed through patience and faith, culminating in charity, as Peter concludes."},{"kind":"para","text":"The most important thing to add to faith, which will enhance all the other areas mentioned, is love or charity. This is not just a superficial “I love you as a brother in Christ” type of love, but a deep “I love you because you are a human being and you are precious to the Lord” type of love. Godly love involves having compassion for others, even those who hate you, and especially for those who do. But why does it matter? The answer lies in our next verse: 8 For if these things be in you, and abound, they make you that ye shall neither be barren nor unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"It gives you more knowledge of Christ. It helps you to grow closer to Him. There are many ways to grow close to God, but faith is the most important way to show God love. There is no great work that a child of God can do than to allow God to work through your faith."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him.","ref":"Hebrews 11:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"That might not be enough for some people. Growing closer to Christ isn’t a real goal for people (though that should be the goal for His children). Some people believe that it’s about more than that. Why would I want to know more about Christ if I am going to just suffer anyway? We’ve pointed out the answer to this before, but I think it’s good to be reminded."},{"kind":"para","text":"Whether with Christ or without Christ, we will experience suffering. It just depends on how you want to suffer. I’d argue that you will suffer more with Christ, but you will have more help through Christ than you would without Him. Which is why Peter has verse 9 for us… 9 But he that lacketh these things is blind, and cannot see afar off, and hath forgotten that he was purged from his old sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter suggests that it is possible to be saved and still lack faith. It is conceivable to have a relationship with Christ and somehow lose sight of Him. When you are not focused on adding to your faith, you become blind and forget what Christ took you from. We must constantly remember where we were before Christ. Knowing that Christ saved you from your old state will guarantee to humble you every time, which will provide you with more love. This love, in turn, provides for your brotherly kindness, which then leads to godliness, patience, temperance, knowledge, virtue, and ultimately your faith. 10 Wherefore the rather, brethren, give diligence to make your calling and election sure: for if ye do these things, ye shall never fall:"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should be making sure we are standing on God’s promises. Maybe we have been distracted from where God had been leading us. Maybe we got off course somehow and need to be humbled about what He did and who He is. We need to add these to our faith. Why? Because “if ye do these things, ye shall NEVER fall.” We fall because we do not do these things… 11 For so an entrance shall be ministered unto you abundantly into the everlasting kingdom of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. 12 Wherefore I will not be negligent to put you always in remembrance of these things, though ye know them, and be established in the present truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"An entrance is given to every single child of God to the everlasting kingdom of God. Peter speaks here, not of the rapture (for Jesus tells him that he will die a martyr in the book of John), but rather of the promise of an eternal home. While Peter still has breath to breathe on Earth, he has made it his goal (as we should make it ours) to remind the saints of their need to grow in faith! 13 Yea, I think it meet, as long as I am in this tabernacle, to stir you up by putting you in remembrance; 14 Knowing that shortly I must put off this my tabernacle, even as our Lord Jesus Christ hath shewed me. 15 Moreover I will endeavour that ye may be able after my decease to have these things always in remembrance."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter knows that his last words must be remembered. If we are to take anything away from Peter’s last words, it should be the importance of faith. He pleads with the readers here to pay attention to the necessities he’s placing because the world is slowly turning away from faith in God and towards idolatry (i.e., themselves). He even says he desires that after he dies, these churches (and us today as readers) would remember the emphasis he has put on increasing faith during difficult turmoil."},{"kind":"para","text":"Remember to have joy. Remember to keep those precious things in which God has entrusted us. Use the trials as directions for something greater!"}]}]},{"start":16,"end":21,"title":"Christ's Glory and the Prophetic Word","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, if Peter and the apostles had been lying about who Christ really was, the stories are exceptionally well rehearsed to the point that dozens of people were lying about the same thing in various forms. Paul’s story of conversion is amazing. The twelve disciples’ stories of conversion are amazing. Even the story of Judas and Jesus is magnificent, and the idea that Jesus lived a perfect life without sin, walking among sinners (even Satan himself enticing him), is an amazing story."},{"kind":"para","text":"But Peter emphasizes that these were not just stories. Peter says, “I saw this all with my own eyes. And that is why I’m able to accurately tell you about it today, and that is why my faith continues to grow stronger every day!” 17 For he received from God the Father honour and glory, when there came such a voice to him from the excellent glory, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. 18 And this voice which came from heaven we heard, when we were with him in the holy mount."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter was labelled as one of Jesus’ three inner circle. It was usually Peter, James, and James’ brother John (the Revelator). Those three seemed to be the most focused on throughout the gospel accounts and saw some of the most incredible things in the ministry with Jesus. This account that Peter speaks of here was one of the most amazing!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And after six days Jesus taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart, And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light. And, behold, there appeared unto them Moses and Elias talking with him. Then answered Peter, and said unto Jesus, Lord, it is good for us to be here: if thou wilt, let us make here three tabernacles; one for thee, and one for Moses, and one for Elias. While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him.","ref":"Matthew 17:1-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"It would be hard to believe that this actually happened had it not been for the other disciples’ accounts of it, and the fact that Peter is attesting to it right here. Matthew’s account had to obviously be a second-hand account. Maybe it was Peter who told him about it. Mark talks about it as well in chapter 9 of Mark’s gospel."},{"kind":"para","text":"This moment was a picture of the coming kingdom. It was a small picture, but it was a peek behind the curtain of what was to come by the promises of God for His children. Peter was really excited about this event, and it’s why he highlights it here. He got a small peek at what he was moving towards, and he couldn’t help himself but to push towards that with as many people as possibly were willing to go with him. 19 We have also a more sure word of prophecy; whereunto ye do well that ye take heed, as unto a light that shineth in a dark place, until the day dawn, and the day star arise in your hearts: 20 Knowing this first, that no prophecy of the scripture is of any private interpretation. 21 For the prophecy came not in old time by the will of man: but holy men of God spake as they were moved by the Holy Ghost."},{"kind":"para","text":"These verses really complement each other. Peter tells us that there is no reason for us to be confused or to question God on the matter of His promises. He has proven it over and over again, not just in Peter’s life, but in the lives of every believer, and proven it through the faith of those who put their trust in Jesus Christ as Savior. If it seems confusing to you about who Christ is, or what the scripture actually says or talks about, you are best to wait for understanding and be proven to the legitimacy of it, rather than taking someone else’s word for it or interpreting it yourself."},{"kind":"para","text":"We hold a complete copy of God’s Word in our hands, and Peter tells us it’s more sure than it ever has been before because God fulfilled all His promises, except for one (which is the end of all things). God moved men to write the scripture. Man did not will themselves to do it. Otherwise, we would have false scripture in our Bibles today. Someone once said, “I know God’s Word is inspired because it inspires me.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Men of God were not physically led to write scripture, but spiritually led to write it. This all ties back into verse 5-7 about adding to our faith. It was through that faith, and what was added to it, that men were moved to write (by God’s Spirit) what we now depend on today. It’s this scripture, by our faith, that changes who we are and what we do. These are not tangible things. Many religions think that you have to give tangibly or you have to physically work your way to Heaven. No."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter himself (who many religions falsely look up to as a perfect leader of the church) says that we cannot add tangible things to our faith in order to increase it. Not one of these additions is something we can physically hold (even brotherly love is not tangible). These are all spiritually led things, and that is the most important part to remember. It’s not by OUR will, but God’s Will that our faith can increase."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":16,"end":16}],"verses":{"1":"Simon Peter, a servant and an apostle of Jesus Christ, to them that have obtained like precious faith with us through the righteousness of God and our Saviour Jesus Christ:","3":"According as his divine power hath given unto us all things that pertain unto life and godliness, through the knowledge of him that hath called us to glory and virtue:","16":"For we have not followed cunningly devised fables, when we made known unto you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but were eyewitnesses of his majesty."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: In chapter 1, we see Peter open this book up with a warning. He has become aware of a devilish presence in the church which is turning people away from truth and righteousness ousness and is taking the time to point it out before it gets any bigger, or digs any deeper in the church. If it does, the church will start to crumble, and there will be much more to worry about than just the false preacher. He wanted you to make sure you were secure in your faith, and that you are affirmed in the promises that God has made for you, because the things he is about to write about will afflict you if you are not."}],"previously":["In chapter 1, we see Peter open this letter up with a warning. He has become aware of a devilish presence in the church which is turning people away from truth and righteousness and is taking the time to point it out before it gets any bigger or digs any deeper into the church. If it does, the church will start to crumble, and there will be much more to worry about than just the false preacher. He wanted to make sure you were secure in your faith and that you are affirmed in the promises that God has made for you, because the things he is about to write about will afflict you if you are not."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":22,"title":"False Prophets and Teachers","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There were false prophets and teachers even in the Old Testament, and they are spoken about as a majority in those days!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now therefore send, and gather to me all Israel unto mount Carmel, and the prophets of Baal four hundred and fifty, and the prophets of the groves four hundred, which eat at Jezebel's table.","ref":"1 Kings 18:19"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is one particular chapter, and just before this, there were 100 prophets of God who were saved through the governor of Obadiah. Obviously, there were a lot more people prophesying for false gods than there were prophesying for the Lord. More than likely, this was because the false gods made them feel better than the true God, who pointed out all of their sins and evil doings. Peter says things have not changed, “…even as there shall be false teachers among you…” We will always have to deal with false teachers and preachers. When America was founded, it was founded on the principle of free worship to God. These Europeans wanted a place to worship the true God where they wouldn’t be condemned or told how to study their own Bibles, but so that they could follow what the Lord actually had written. I do believe that is why America still exists today. God gave us favor."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, because we have an open practice of religion in this country, it didn’t take long for cults and false worshipers to make excuses to try and overthrow worship here for the true God. Satanism has risen substantially here, and only because we have allowed the practice of free worship. No other country has this level of false worship and variety, and no other country will ever have this level of false worship because no other country will allow it. This is part of the reason why I believe the Antichrist will come from America. But that’s a topic for a different time."},{"kind":"para","text":"The real question is, how do we differentiate what is right doctrine from what is false doctrine? How can we tell who is teaching rightly and who is teaching falsely? There’s a key to this, and Peter will open up what to look for, as well as Paul in 1 & 2 Timothy, and John in 1, 2, & 3 John. It is very difficult to find the false teachers because they all have a small portion of true doctrine thrown in with the bad. They do this on purpose to successfully twist the Word of God. This is why your faith must be your own, and not that of someone else who is teaching you. 2 And many shall follow their pernicious ways; by reason of whom the way of truth shall be evil spoken of."},{"kind":"para","text":"Curiously enough, those enticed by false doctrine will eventually find reason to call Christ and His ways “evil.” They won’t have a kind word to say about Christianity or the Bible. They will ultimately become like their teacher, following a man’s logic rather than their own. They won’t think about it themselves; they want it spoon-fed to them, and there will always be worldly people carrying spoons to feed the lost sheep that are not willing to follow the Lord. That should be the ultimate reminder to the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are nothing more than sheep. Yet we have thought of ourselves as more and try to play it off like we can become sheep herders, pastors, preachers, or teachers. We are sheep. Even the ones in charge are sheep. We follow only one shepherd, and that is the Lord of the harvest."},{"kind":"para","text":"3 And through covetousness shall they with feigned words make merchandise of you: whose judgment now of a long time lingereth not, and their damnation slumbereth not."},{"kind":"para","text":"False teachers and their followers will try to exploit you. Honestly, and this is something we have to come to terms with, the world is looking at you and trying to find something wrong so they can call you a hypocrite. They are picking your life apart, either trying to hold something from your past against you or something from the present, so they can say, “See, nothing has changed. She or he is still the same person as before. Still the same sinner. Still the same profane, terrible, and worldly person that I knew in the past.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you have become a Christian, you have put yourself and your life into a glass box, and everyone is watching you. Why? Not because they want to follow you. Not even because they want what you want (there are very few people who will see your life and desire to live it). No, they want to see who you are to see if it’s genuine. They want to try and pin something to you."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve actually had people say, “Oh, you’re a Christian” or “You’re a teacher or leader of the Bible? I’m going to be watching to see when you mess up.” And I’ve always told them, “You won’t have to wait very long to see that happen.” I am still fleshly, but I am ever seeking. Still fleshly, but striving spiritually. Still fleshly, but anticipating the otherworldly. Still fleshly, but resistant to the flesh. The rest of the world knows it’s fleshly, but they cling to it. They enjoy it, and they don’t desire change. I didn’t either before I was a Christian. I liked my sin. I didn’t want to be noticed for standing out. Even as a Christian, I don’t want to be noticed."},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, God notices me, and He sends people my way who suddenly notice me. I don’t want to be noticed, but I now notice everyone. There’s a whole world that needs the truth, and they are being fed lies all the time. God has commissioned us to simply tell the truth among all the false teachers and be prepared to be made “merchandise.” We will be exploited if we desire to stand against the false teachers of our day. But it will be worth it if there is only one person who comes to know the truth because you said something. 4 For if God spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment; 5 And spared not the old world, but saved Noah the eighth person, a preacher of righteousness, bringing in the flood upon the world of the ungodly; 6 And turning the cities of Sodom and Gomorrha into ashes condemned them with an overthrow, making them an ensample unto those that after should live ungodly;"},{"kind":"para","text":"God has proven Himself over and over again in times past. He doesn’t even treat the angels specially. He enacts righteousness and judgment equally to all creation. Honestly, we have it easier than the angels do. God has allowed us repentance. There’s not one word about the angels having the opportunity to repent. This tells me that their choice of serving God or serving Satan is final. There’s no going back to the Lord for them. Whereas, we have a struggle between the two, and God gives multiple chances because He’s not willing that ANY should perish. God’s Word is a beacon of continual chances and righteous judgment. It shows the standard in which God has, and the standard in which the world lives by. It shows us how little we actually are in the grand scheme of things when we think we have obtained all knowledge and all understanding."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are so small compared to what God wants us to know. Even those things we think we know about eternity are such surface-level that they can’t be compared to what it will actually be like. We can follow this teaching and that teaching. We can understand this idea or that concept. We can put faith into one sermon or multiple sermons. Even in the Bible, we are not given the full picture because it would be impossible for us to grasp our minds around. Just the book of Revelation alone is so fantastical and so otherworldly that there is a lot of debate about what it actually says and what it actually means. This proves we cannot even contain the knowledge that God has stored for us in Heaven, which I do believe we will tap into when we get there."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even something like the flood of Noah or the fall of Sodom and Gomorrah has been greatly misunderstood. These are basic ideas of someone who understands salvation and scripture, but for those who don’t know, they find these things disgusting acts of terror. “Why would a loving God flood the Earth or kill thousands of people in cities who were just living their lives?” It doesn’t seem that God is very loving if you continuously put Him out of context like false teachers try to do. When people say this and quote scripture from these parts, they ultimately admit that they are okay with rape, murder, stealing, lying, enslavement, brutality, homelessness, and the such as there were during that time. It was nearly the ENTIRE WORLD acting as this during the time of Noah (which is why Noah was the only one to survive), and it was nearly the entire cities of Sodom and Gomorrah acting like this (which is why it was only Lot and his family that survived). Peter continues… 7 And delivered just Lot, vexed with the filthy conversation of the wicked: 8 (For that righteous man dwelling among them, in seeing and hearing, vexed his righteous soul from day to day with their unlawful deeds;)"},{"kind":"para","text":"Apparently, Lot was “vexed” in the city. He hated being there when he was there. He didn’t desire to stay, but he felt stuck. He lost most of his family and was entrenched in the politics of that day and place. If you read through the account of Lot and his family—his wife looking back and turning into a pillar of salt, and then what happens with his daughters—you wouldn’t think of Lot as a “saved” man. Saved from the angels’ wrath, but not saved for eternity’s sake."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter describes him differently, however, and says that if God was able to save Lot (who according to Peter was a righteous man), then God will know how to save the Godly today! These Old Testament stories serve as examples for us today. They are examples of God’s leading, care, and ultimate goal for His children. They are examples to prove that God never leaves us nor forsakes us. They prove that God is constantly looking out for His own and keeps track of all the terrible things the world tries to throw their way. These are examples and proofs to show that God cares! 9 The Lord knoweth how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment to be punished:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Some are under the impression that we are currently going through the Great Tribulation period, and I won’t accept or reject the idea. However, Peter makes a very good point here to remind the readers that God knows how to deliver His own. It is crazier to me than knowing that God instructs and pulls us in directions we need to be in for the ultimate good that Peter says God reserves “the unjust unto the day of judgement to be punished.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"God waits for His ultimate punishment of the unjust. I cannot tell the difference between the unjust and the godly today. It seems to be getting harder and harder the longer I am a Christian. They blend in so well. God tells us to let the wheat and tares grow together, and He will have no problem sorting them out later. Peter reminds us that God knows exactly what to do with both sides when the time comes. 10 But chiefly them that walk after the flesh in the lust of uncleanness, and despise government. Presumptuous are they, selfwilled, they are not afraid to speak evil of dignities."},{"kind":"para","text":"The people, who are false teachers, are entrenched in defiling themselves and despising authority. Many Christians cannot stand the current authority. I believe they have more of a right to despise authority than the rest of the world. We understand what is right and wrong because of the standard set before us, whereas the world has made up their own version of what is right and what is wrong."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, we are told over and over again to love authority, respect authority, and even Peter has mentioned this before."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let every soul be subject unto the higher powers. For there is no power but of God: the powers that be are ordained of God.","ref":"Romans 13:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Obey them that have the rule over you, and submit yourselves: for they watch for your souls, as they that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you.","ref":"Hebrews 13:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Servants, be subject to your masters with all fear; not only to the good and gentle, but also to the froward. For this is thankworthy, if a man for conscience toward God endure grief, suffering wrongfully. For what glory is it, if, when ye be buffeted for your faults, ye shall take it patiently? but if, when ye do well, and suffer for it, ye take it patiently, this is acceptable with God.","ref":"1 Peter 2:18-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"Does this mean we should follow through with ungodly rulership and listen to what they have to say as far as leading your life against the Lord? NO! Of course not! But we should not give them a reason or excuse as to why they should be against us or cause us harm. We should strive to live peaceably with all men. But we should also say as Peter…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"…We ought to obey God rather than men.","ref":"Acts 5:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"11 Whereas angels, which are greater in power and might, bring not railing accusation against them before the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter states that angels possess far more power than we do and are obviously closer to God. They do not even attempt to speak evil of others. They are not concerned with speaking evil against anyone, even if we have truly done something wrong. Why? Because they know that God will deal with it."},{"kind":"para","text":"In fact, in Jude 9 (a passage worth studying further), an angel and Satan had a conversation about what to do with the body of Moses. Satan did not want Moses to appear later at the transfiguration of Jesus in the promised land, so he tried to keep the body out of it, but God buried the body. Jude says in chapter 9 that the angel simply told Satan, “THE LORD rebuke thee.” The angel had the power to put Satan in his place, but instead, he just gave it over to God."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a lesson we should all learn, especially if we are followers of Christ. Rather than taking the fight into our own hands, we should allow the Lord to give direction. Sometimes that means the fight will be given to us, but other times it means that God will deal with it in His own time. 12 But these, as natural brute beasts, made to be taken and destroyed, speak evil of the things that they understand not; and shall utterly perish in their own corruption; 13 And shall receive the reward of unrighteousness, as they that count it pleasure to riot in the day time. Spots they are and blemishes, sporting themselves with their own deceivings while they feast with you;"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter doesn’t sugarcoat these false teachers. He calls them as they are. In Peter’s eyes, they are “beasts,” willing and waiting to destroy God’s church and people. This is how we should view them still today. They are reckless with God’s church and they look to try and devour the flock."},{"kind":"para","text":"Can I just say (in case you haven’t gotten this picture yet from Peter), the beasts and false teachers are not those of other churches. Peter is warning this church about the leadership within ITSELF! In other words, you will experience false teachers and doctrine within your own body if you are not cautious and careful. It doesn’t even necessarily have to be amongst the leadership. Satan can attack even through the congregation. I’ve seen it happen on both sides before."},{"kind":"para","text":"Too much charge from the leadership gives Satan an entrance into the church. Too much charge from the congregation gives Satan an entrance into the church. Too little of either gives Satan an entrance into the church. The pastor must learn how to balance this issue in order for the church to flourish. If the leader cannot, it will only be a matter of time before Satan runs his course, causing the leadership and the congregation to become beasts waiting to be fed, and feeding things that are ungodly."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter does mention that they will suffer for their wrongdoing, however, and this is simply because they think they are pleasing the Lord in what they are doing. They live by the motto “nothing moves forward without friction.” This is a very good saying, and it’s true, as long as it’s moving forward. Many who follow this motto think they are moving forward when in actuality they are sliding downhill with it and call THAT “friction.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"They are usually the most prideful people, saying “I am suffering for the Lord.” When in actuality they are just suffering for themselves and because of themselves. They have no idea what true Godly suffering really is. Peter continues to label them… 14 Having eyes full of adultery, and that cannot cease from sin; beguiling unstable souls: an heart they have exercised with covetous practices; cursed children:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, Peter hits the nail on the head with each statement, but the one that stood out to me the most was “beguiling unstable souls.” This could refer to a range of people. It includes those who were once hurt by the church, those who were hurt by “Christian” parents, those who were hurt by “Christian” friends, and those who were once on fire for the Lord, whose fire was put out because of bad leadership. These souls are unstable. They are more unstable than those who have not known the Lord at all."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many times, these hurt souls are left confused and grasping for any kind of Godly comfort they can get, and that is often why they fall into a trap of false teaching and bad doctrine. They want to feel better about their faith choices. Churches should be able to do that for people, but sadly, many churches don’t. So people get vacuumed into false churches because the false church likes to “beguile” unstable souls. They end up leading God’s sheep astray, and eventually, they are lost forever because of these false preachers."},{"kind":"para","text":"You should never join a church solely on feeling. Look to the doctrine first, and then search the Spirit. If it all aligns to God’s Word, then it’s a blessed church to join. If it does not, then (though I (and probably Peter) could find more colorful words to call them) they are “cursed children.” 15 Which have forsaken the right way, and are gone astray, following the way of Balaam the son of Bosor, who loved the wages of unrighteousness; 16 But was rebuked for his iniquity: the dumb ass speaking with man's voice forbad the madness of the prophet."},{"kind":"para","text":"“The way of Balaam” was a covetous work, where religious exercises were done for personal profit. There are many in the church today who fall under this category. I’ve had many atheists and agnostics alike call church “big business,” and judging by some mega-churches, they aren’t always wrong. But if your sole intent for serving God is for a paycheck, I’d rather you leave the church today. Don’t teach my children. Don’t teach my family. Stay away from the pulpit and stage. Stay away from preaching the gospel and soul-winning. You are doing nothing but trying to cause error on the part of others."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, I’ve seen this side of the church before. The congregation would even say, “Well, we pay you to go soul-winning, and we pay YOU to preach God’s Word and to sing worship music, and to teach our kids.” I understand the necessity to pay the staff of a church, for there is no price higher than the souls of the people in this world. In fact, the price was ultimately paid for by Christ, and those who lead through Christ lead others who owe Christ a debt they could never afford to pay except through praise and worship."},{"kind":"para","text":"Leaders of the church should NEVER be paid to lead. They should be solemnly paid to serve. They should be paid in love and respect, care and compassion, tangibly and intangibly. Leaders who lead through Christ simply to serve people are worthy of payment for such. Leaders who respect the paycheck more than the congregation should be sent out of the church immediately. This might be a harsh topic, but we are told to watch out for these people over and over again. 17 These are wells without water, clouds that are carried with a tempest; to whom the mist of darkness is reserved for ever."},{"kind":"para","text":"A well without water isn’t much of anything, is it? We can have a hole in the ground and still be thirsty. It can be the best-kept and greatest-looking well that this earth has ever seen, but if it doesn’t have water, what is the point of it?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve seen many “fake” wells in LA. We were visiting a little street called “Olvera,” where a bunch of little shops sold Mexican trinkets and clothes. There were two wells there that were fake. Neither of them went very deep, and neither of them had water. People still, for some reason, threw money in them. It wouldn’t have been very difficult for me to pick up the money, that’s how shallow the wells were. I didn’t understand the purpose of the wells."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I still don’t understand, but I think (based on this verse) I have my guess. They probably anticipated visitors throwing money in. That’s all they were made for. Whoever made them would come by and pick up the money and exchange it for cash. That is what Peter is referring to false preachers as here. He says they are shallow wells, which are good for nothing but to collect your money. 18 For when they speak great swelling words of vanity, they allure through the lusts of the flesh, through much wantonness, those that were clean escaped from them who live in error."},{"kind":"para","text":"I like to think that most false teachers are very cunning in the way they speak. They are so convincing and well-rehearsed that they even fool themselves into believing what they are preaching without a second thought. They probably haven’t looked much deeper than what they know to be honest. They claim to know the scripture, but they truly only know it at a surface level and have not taken the time to dive deep into God’s Word to obtain the amazing pearls it has to offer. They have taken God’s Word at face value and have made up their own story because it sounds better."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, sometimes it does sound better. Who doesn’t want to be told that they will become monetarily rich if they just live for God? Who doesn’t want to be told that they can feel better if they just wear a suit and tie and act like they already have the money they need? Who doesn’t want to be told that there is a miraculous healing for their difficulties and health concerns and that if they just pay a certain amount of money, the preacher will hit them with his jacket, touch them, or speak in tongues?"},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s all lies, though. It’s all fake so that they can fill their pockets with your money. It’s difficult to escape from such amazing deceit because you desperately want God to make it true. This is idolatry, and false preachers play into this all the time."},{"kind":"para","text":"19 While they promise them liberty, they themselves are the servants of corruption: for of whom a man is overcome, of the same is he brought in bondage."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will be overcome by something. We will always serve something. We will either serve God or ourselves. God or Satan. God or mammon."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"No man can serve two masters: for either he will hate the one, and love the other; or else he will hold to the one, and despise the other. Ye cannot serve God and mammon.","ref":"Matthew 6:24"},{"kind":"para","text":"God or false gods? God or fear? God or the world?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And if it seem evil unto you to serve the LORD, choose you this day whom ye will serve; whether the gods which your fathers served that were on the other side of the flood, or the gods of the Amorites, in whose land ye dwell: but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD.","ref":"Joshua 24:15 20 For if after they have escaped the pollutions of the world through the knowledge of the Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ, they are again entangled therein, and overcome, the latter end is worse with them than the beginning. 21 For it had been better for them not to have known the way of righteousness, than, after they have known it, to turn from the holy commandment delivered unto them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Question: Is it possible to be saved and then to turn from God and live against Him?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter seems to think it is possible. But you have to wonder, “If they were really saved, can they ever go against God and the knowledge of truth?” I think we have a great number of pastors and preachers who teach a doctrine called “backsliding.” I won’t say anything against or for this doctrine, but I will say it leaves many to wonder about the legitimacy of the faith in many lives of self-proclaimed Christians. If they could easily fall away from the Lord, they didn’t seem to be very secure in the Lord to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter seems to also think that if you do end up falling away from the Lord after you were saved, it will be worse for you than those who never even knew God to begin with. Why? I don’t know. Peter doesn’t illustrate why. I have my guesses, and most of them revolve around you being the face of hypocrisy. 22 But it is happened unto them according to the true proverb, The dog is turned to his own vomit again; and the sow that was washed to her wallowing in the mire."},{"kind":"para","text":"What a great picture to end this chapter on. This is a quote from Proverbs."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"As a dog returneth to his vomit, so a fool returneth to his folly.","ref":"Proverbs 26:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Rumor has it that a dog’s mouth is cleaner than a human’s mouth. Those who have spread this rumor must not own a dog. I have seen dogs do some pretty disgusting things that I won’t share simply for your own mental imagery’s sake. This trait, of dogs returning to their own vomit, is a common dog trait. It’s disgusting, but the reason that dogs do this is because they aren’t getting the proper nutrients from the food they are eating. They think that their own vomit has more nutrients in it, so they go and eat it to obtain what they are lacking."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is the picture that Peter is giving here to false preachers. They are ones who think that God’s Word doesn’t have enough nutrients in it, so they eat filth and garbage, and they throw it up because it doesn’t sit well with them (because it’s fake and artificial), and then they think that the best way to be filled back up is by eating it again. May we be cautious about what we are taking in and what we are giving out. May it be nutrients for the growth of God’s people so they don’t have to return to their own vomit."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1}],"verses":{"1":"But there were false prophets also among the people, even as there shall be false teachers among you, who privily shall bring in damnable heresies, even denying the Lord that bought them, and bring upon themselves swift destruction."}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Tuesday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: Complete"},{"kind":"para","text":"Previously: Peter had some seriously strong wording for false teachers in our last chapter. He didn’t hold anything back, and he recommended to the saints that were reading the letter to stay clear of these evil workers of satan. His anger and accusations of these bad teachers was justified for his love of the people, and concern over weak/new faith children of God. The worst thing a teacher (or any child of God for that matter) can ever do is lead others poorly."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this closing chapter, Peter is concluding his thoughts, and giving encouragement to those that take his warnings and cautions seriously. He wants those (that are true to the faith) to be aware of the situation, and proceed in their walks and fellowship with one another with caution. Considering their own faith and making their “calling and election sure.”"}],"previously":["Peter had some seriously strong wording for false teachers in our last chapter. He didn’t hold anything back, and he recommended to the saints that were reading the letter to stay clear of these evil workers of Satan. His anger and accusations of these bad teachers were justified for his love of the people, and concern over weak/new faith children of God. The worst thing a teacher (or any child of God for that matter) can ever do is lead others poorly. In this closing chapter, Peter is concluding his thoughts, and giving encouragement to those that take his warnings and cautions seriously. He wants those (that are true to the faith) to be aware of the situation, and proceed in their walks and fellowship with one another with caution. Considering their own faith and making their “calling and election sure.”"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Encouragement to Wait for The Day of the Lord","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We can verily figure out the purposes of the letters that were written by Peter and assume, based on our study time, that he was writing to help those who would accept it. But in case it wasn’t enough for you to study it with that obvious conclusion, Peter sums up the purpose for writing the letter in this verse here. I honestly like the term he uses. He says, “I’m writing, not just because I want to hurt those that are hurting others (like many pastors today desire to do), but I’m writing because I want you to be ‘stirred’ up!” There’s a difference between good and bad stirring. Bad stirring leaves a bitter taste to those you are speaking to. If you stir up with a fleshly attitude, or you stir up with the intent to tear down and destroy, you are stirring up heresy."},{"kind":"para","text":"The opposite of that, however, is stirring up with the right attitude, and the goal of loving compassion towards those who need it breeds passion! It breeds fire! Peter could say it this way, “I am writing this letter to stir your pure minds so that you can be on FIRE for the Lord! Rather than seeing everyone lie and cheat their way to the top, take this as an opportunity to do what’s right and have a ‘let’s get this done’ type of mentality.” If you’ve ever been on fire for the Lord, you know what this is like."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was first saved, I couldn’t get enough. I wanted to do everything. I thought everyone was silly or fake Christians if they weren’t on fire as much as me. They were scared to talk to others? They must not be on fire! They were missing church services and gatherings? They must not be on fire! They were letting little things keep them from studying their Bibles every day (even more than once a day)? They must not be on fire! I had a lot of maturing to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"That same fire, however, is something we should strive for. Not the pridefulness at looking down at others who are not matching your faith and walk, but the passion to serve God as much as possible because you can’t get enough! Many people are hurt by the church, or hurt by other Christians, sometimes even bad leadership, and that keeps them back from serving, when in actuality, as Peter says, this should stir us up!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter wasn’t just stirring up the others; he was stirring himself up too! He was learning with them in this process! He was seeing their faith dwindle because of the lack of feeding, or because of the hurt and trials they were facing. He didn’t want that to dwindle, and if no one else was going to stir up those white-hot coals and add a little fuel to the fire of their faith, it would be him while he was experiencing the pain of his biggest trial in life."},{"kind":"para","text":"In approximately 300 AD, there was a man by the name of Eusebius who did an extensive study on the apostles of scripture. He was a well-known historian and was fascinated by learning about the ministries that the apostles took place in after the biblical accounts. He took very special care to find the most concise resources available during the time of some trusted scholars who were taking account of the happenings and locations of each apostle. It’s a fascinating study of his life if you desire to do some digging later. The point I want to focus on is one of his studies on the Apostle Peter’s martyrdom. Remember, this letter was more than likely written around the time of his imprisonment and just merely weeks before he was killed."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is well noted throughout the scriptures that Peter was married and had a family. Children? I don’t know. But certainly a wife. Paul even writes about this in 1 Corinthians."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Have we not power to lead about a sister, a wife, as well as other apostles, and as the brethren of the Lord, and Cephas?","ref":"1 Corinthians 9:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"In other words, it seems the apostles’ wives were often traveling with them. Cephas is the name of Peter, as we should know at this point…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he brought him to Jesus. And when Jesus beheld him, he said, Thou art Simon the son of Jona: thou shalt be called Cephas, which is by interpretation, A stone.","ref":"John 1:42"},{"kind":"para","text":"It was noted that Peter’s wife was likely with him in prison for the same purposes of faith. Eusebius quotes from Clement of Alexandria, one of his trusted sources, saying, “…when the blessed Peter saw his own wife led out to die, he rejoiced because of her summons and her return home, and called to her very encouragingly and comfortingly, addressing her by name, and says, ‘oh thou, remember the Lord.’”"},{"kind":"para","text":"I imagine that his letters to the churches just before this were his encouragement to be an example during this difficult scene in his life. How terrible of a feeling it would be in my heart and soul to see my wife led out before me, knowing she is about to be put to death for her faith. Yet, how encouraging it is to know that she will no longer have to suffer through the pains and heartaches of this sinful world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter’s soul was being stirred while writing this letter, and it meant there was ample reason to stir up those who would read it."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter emphasizes the importance of remembering something in the coming verse. He highlights this matter because it was a topic discussed by Old Testament preachers and has been consistently preached by the other apostles, or his brothers in Christ, with whom he has spent a great deal of time. 3 Knowing this first, that there shall come in the last days scoffers, walking after their own lusts,"},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter is not just referring to the last days as something off in the distant future. He’s saying to the readers of this letter that this is applicable today. The last days commenced during the giving of the Holy Ghost. We are currently living in the last days."},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t hold to the idea that we are living in the day of Great Tribulation, but we are certainly living in the days before Christ’s return! The time will come when the Lord will descend and take His church from off this Earth, and He will deal with the rest as He knows how to best."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord. Wherefore comfort one another with these words.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:16-18"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are to keep these thoughts in mind when we experience these difficulties. They will scoff, and they will be so enamored with themselves, thinking so highly of themselves. It will be easy to spot those who will try and make fun of you for your faith in this old book. They will think you are crazy for listening to ancient writings, while they continue to put their trust into the worst kinds of filth imaginable (all ancient as well, comparative to their age, thinking of Darwin, or the Quran, the Talmud, or any other kind of false religious text). They won’t just stop at scoffing; Peter addresses some of the things they will say…"},{"kind":"para","text":"4 And saying, Where is the promise of his coming? for since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation."},{"kind":"para","text":"They still make this comment today. The accusation is the same. They think, “They’ve been claiming the return of the Lord for generations now, and He still isn’t here. Nothing has changed, why do you still believe this lie?” We can certainly have some choice words to give them. However, we can use logic and proof, which many will not believe or listen to. We can provide them with facts and cold-hard evidence, and they will still find reasons to brush it off as if it’s not enough. Peter addresses this in the best way he can in the rest of this chapter…"}]}]},{"start":5,"end":6,"title":"Past World","entries":[{"start":5,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Peter addresses the hard evidence of the old world, the world that “perished” with a flood of some sort. It is assumed, and I am under the same line of thinking, that this is speaking about the flood of Noah’s day. There was a cultural shift after the flood came. Though we are still stuck in our sins, the world certainly was not the same after Noah’s lineage. Not to mention the evidence that is still being found today."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’m told there was a great shaft put down at the ancient site of Ur of the Chaldees, which shows that there were several civilizations destroyed. In the excavation, the archaeologists found a lot of sand and silt with quite a bit of sediment deposited there by a flood. Then beneath all of that, they found the remains of a very high civilization. This is one of many examples we could track if we so desire to prove the great flood that many today have a hard time grasping or believing in (take them to the Grand Canyon; that’s a pretty significant example)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter addresses the fact that the people during Noah’s day were no different than the people today. For 150 years while Noah preached about the coming judgment of God, people probably said the same thing: “Where is the promise of his coming? For since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of creation.” Then it started to rain…"}]}]},{"start":7,"end":12,"title":"Present World","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Hey, you know why you are still alive today? Because God has preserved you. He preserves the Earth as well. He is waiting for a specific day and time when He will have no choice but to destroy all things here. When that event occurs, God will do away with the current generations living and abominations, and Earth will be completely different."},{"kind":"para","text":"But people will say the same thing they did in Noah’s day! It’s been 2000 years, they will say, “Where is the promise of his coming? For since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation.” Then it will start to rain balls of fire. So where’s the answer to this question? Where is the Lord? I believe it’s found in the next verse…"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This doesn’t state that God has no grasp on time. We could get in a lot of trouble assuming that God is stupid enough not to have a simple and basic understanding of time (something He created). This is to say that God is patient."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"O give thanks unto the God of gods: for his mercy endureth for ever.","ref":"Psalm 136:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Is it that His mercy endures until it can’t anymore? No. Is it that His mercy endures for as long as necessary? No. Is it that His mercy endures for a while? No. His mercy endures forever! That’s inspired scripture that says that! It says that His mercy has no end. He does not have the desire to destroy the Earth, nor kill a single person. He desires that all would repent! Which is why Peter says this next! 9 The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why hasn’t Christ returned yet? Because there are still people who have the chance to be saved! As long as there are people worth saving, God has reason to wait. The moment there are none willing to turn to the Lord anymore will be the moment Christ will return…"},{"kind":"para","text":"“But WHEN though?!” You are frantically asking. I don’t know. “During the tribulation?” Maybe? “Before the tribulation?” Maybe? “After the tribulation, and just before it’s over?” Maybe… Does it matter? Does knowing the time of Christ’s return really matter to your mission for Him? Is the mission still not the same? Peter once again addresses this question, because he knows that the readers will ask him when the Lord will return."},{"kind":"para","text":"10 But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night; in the which the heavens shall pass away with a great noise, and the elements shall melt with fervent heat, the earth also and the works that are therein shall be burned up."},{"kind":"para","text":"In other-words, no one knows, and it will happen when you aren’t expecting it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For yourselves know perfectly that the day of the Lord so cometh as a thief in the night.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Many have the belief today that we were started by an accident of two atoms colliding, ultimately causing an explosion that created all the Earth. We live in a society where the impossible seemingly becomes possible. For instance, it was once impossible to split an atom. The moment that took place, we discovered a new form of creation. We now have the ability to create a bomb that is more powerful than anything this world has seen. I imagine the picture here that Peter is taking into account is much like that, maybe on a grander scale, seeing as an atom bomb is but a little thing to the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter uses more significant scientific words to describe this picture: “Pass away,” “Great noise,” “Elements,” “melt with fervent heat.” The very thing that many “scientists” today believe created all things by accident is the same thing that can level and destroy entire countries by one push of a button. The Lord will have a much bigger explosion than we know possible, and it will melt away the entire Earth. 11 Seeing then that all these things shall be dissolved, what manner of persons ought ye to be in all holy conversation and godliness, 12 Looking for and hasting unto the coming of the day of God, wherein the heavens being on fire shall be dissolved, and the elements shall melt with fervent heat?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Verses 11 and 12 are a question. I like answering the questions that the Bible asks. He says, “if you know all of this is going to happen, and you don’t know the time in which the Lord is coming, what should you be doing right now?” Should we be questioning and waiting for the Lord to reveal His ultimate destruction? Should we be worried about what side of the fence you will be on when it happens? Do you think you should be concerned about how long the Lord is taking to make up His mind on the matter of utter destruction of all things? Or do you, perhaps, think it would be a fine time to continue to do the Lord’s command for you in being FAITHFUL to God’s work like Peter has purposefully focused on over and over again through these letters?"},{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn’t matter when the Lord comes back. What matters is what you are doing for Him until He does! Which is why he continues his letter with this…"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":18,"title":"Future World","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It will be great when there is no more sin, evil, pride, hypocrisy, and lies on this Earth! We certainly look forward to that promise. We look forward to only seeing righteousness here. But until then, we wait with the fire to preach and serve like we never have before!"}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let the thought of the Lord’s return give you motivation to serve Him stronger, rather than slow you down. If knowing His return is eminent doesn’t rev your engine, or switch your gears, or whatever other car analogy you desire to use here, then nothing ever will, and you may have to wonder how strong your faith really is. Others will see your lack of service and wonder as well. 15 And account that the longsuffering of our Lord is salvation; even as our beloved brother Paul also according to the wisdom given unto him hath written unto you;"},{"kind":"para","text":"I like that term, “longsuffering.” It’s another word for patience. My pastor said it was a word that means, “slow to boil.” I’ve heard people break this word down in multiple fashions, and it never feels like it’s explained deeply enough. I like to just break the word up and say, “to suffer long.” I feel that fits the best, especially compared to how Peter is writing. The Lord gives us the ability to not just be patient. He gives us the ability to thrive in a long season of suffering. Only Christians have that power. Why? Because only God has the ability to suffer long."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is the only one truly suffering here, because He sees the sin of the world, and desires that they would simply look to Him for forgiveness. Yet much of His creation doesn’t, and that hurts Him more than anything. Atheists think that the greatest affliction they can bring to God is their lack of belief in Him. That’s not true. God knows (as all of us do) that they believe in God (if they didn’t, why do they hate Him so much?). Rather, the thing that afflicts God the most is their rejection of His salvation. Peter then addressed the longsuffering of Paul…"},{"kind":"para","text":"16 As also in all his epistles, speaking in them of these things; in which are some things hard to be understood, which they that are unlearned and unstable wrest, as they do also the other scriptures, unto their own destruction."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter and Paul had some difficulties with each other at some point…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But when Peter was come to Antioch, I withstood him to the face, because he was to be blamed. For before that certain came from James, he did eat with the Gentiles: but when they were come, he withdrew and separated himself, fearing them which were of the circumcision. And the other Jews dissembled likewise with him; insomuch that Barnabas also was carried away with their dissimulation. But when I saw that they walked not uprightly according to the truth of the gospel, I said unto Peter before them all, If thou, being a Jew, livest after the manner of Gentiles, and not as do the Jews, why compellest thou the Gentiles to live as do the Jews?","ref":"Galatians 2:11-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, something was fixed at some point because there are words of kindness shared with each other later on. Maybe it’s because they are both so close to death that there is no point in bad blood anymore. Maybe it was something else; I really don’t know. But Peter makes it a point to show that Paul’s writing to these churches was scriptural, and they were embedded with absolute truths that would make anyone who would deny his writings an utter fool. I would venture to say the same about Peter’s letters as well. 17 Ye therefore, beloved, seeing ye know these things before, beware lest ye also, being led away with the error of the wicked, fall from your own stedfastness."},{"kind":"para","text":"“I’ve warned you,” Peter says. I’ve often thought about this with my own salvation. I thank the Lord for showing me the truth and opening my eyes to His gift for me. But I think, “If no one had shown me the truth, then I would have an excuse for continuing in my sin.” The truth is, we have all partaken in the chastisement of God through conviction of the law written on our hearts."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But if ye be without chastisement, whereof all are partakers, then are ye bastards, and not sons.","ref":"Hebrews 12:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even more so now, someone has revealed to me what my conscience has been warning me about all along, and now I have no excuse at all for my sins or way of thinking! Even the proof all around me of His creation gives me ample evidence of a Creator who created me!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made, even his eternal power and Godhead; so that they are without excuse:","ref":"Romans 1:20"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are desperately without excuse if we still fall for the same lies and traps that sinful men may try to entice us with because each of us reading through this letter has been warned! If we fall for the traps and deceit of Satan, we cannot blame the Lord, nor Peter, because he’s already proven to us what we must look out for!"},{"kind":"para","text":"18 But grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To him be glory both now and for ever. Amen."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s the essence of the letter! I love this verse! What do we do? Many of us who have been in the faith for an extended amount of time think that there is not too much more to really pull from the Bible. We need something new and fresh, deeper meat to chew on. We need something biblically alluring to keep us going and catch our minds and thoughts. But Peter points out something far more concerning and important in the church today: our growth in grace."},{"kind":"para","text":"We are lacking grace today. Grace is being able to love people who hate you. It’s being able to be calm and caring to people who are purposely trying to hurt you. Grace is the ability to take basic truths from God’s Word and be astounded by them over and over again. We should never tire of the simple things in His Word. They should refresh us every time we read them!"},{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn’t have to be deep or extravagant to get our attention. You just need to learn to grow in grace. I don’t think any of us here would be crazy enough to say, “I have enough grace, I don’t need to grow in that area anymore.” If you believe that, then you have proven your lack of grace and how much more you genuinely need! If we grow more in our grace, we will grow more in our knowledge of Christ. He is the embodiment of grace. That will ultimately bring Him glory and honor."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":5,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14}],"verses":{"1":"This second epistle, beloved, I now write unto you; in both which I stir up your pure minds by way of remembrance:","2":"That ye may be mindful of the words which were spoken before by the holy prophets, and of the commandment of us the apostles of the Lord and Saviour:","5":"For this they willingly are ignorant of, that by the word of God the heavens were of old, and the earth standing out of the water and in the water:","6":"Whereby the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished:","7":"But the heavens and the earth, which are now, by the same word are kept in store, reserved unto fire against the day of judgment and perdition of ungodly men.","8":"But, beloved, be not ignorant of this one thing, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day.","13":"Nevertheless we, according to his promise, look for new heavens and a new earth, wherein dwelleth righteousness.","14":"Wherefore, beloved, seeing that ye look for such things, be diligent that ye may be found of him in peace, without spot, and blameless."}},
  },
};
